kosha ice

  -- H2O in solid phase. stable below 0°C at 1 atm.
  -- hexagonal crystal structure: oxygen atoms in hexagonal rings.
  -- hydrogen bonds hold the structure — open lattice, less dense than liquid water.
  -- density: 917 kg/m³ (ice) vs 1000 kg/m³ (water) — ice floats.
  -- this anomaly is critical for life: frozen lakes insulate below, not freeze solid.
  --
  -- properties:
  --   solid: definite shape and volume
  --   transparent to translucent (depends on air bubbles)
  --   coefficient of restitution: ~0.3 (brittle fracture at high impact)
  --   thermal conductivity: 2.2 W/mK (conducts better than liquid water)
  --   very low friction (especially near 0°C — thin liquid film at surface)
  --   melts at 0°C: absorbs 334 kJ/kg latent heat

